.TH COMMANDS  1R
.UC 4
.SH NAME
cat, chmod, cp, diff, ln, ls, mkdir, mv, rm, rmdir \- remote access version
.SH SYNOPSIS
Same as those in UNIX Programmer's Manual (1).
.SH DESCRIPTION
The above 10 commands have the same meanings as the corresponding
commands described in UNIX Programmer's Manual (1), except that they
accept remote as well as local file names as arguments.  The file name
is in the form of
.I [host:]path.
.PP
If
.I host:
is missing, file name searching starts either from
the root (if
.I path
starts with '/') or from the current working directory.
If
.I
host:
is present, the file name searching starts either from the root (if
.I path
starts with '/') or from the user's home directory in that machine.
There are no current working directories in remote machines.
.I Host:path
can also be used if
.I host
is the local machine.  In this case the
.I path,
if not starting with '/', refers to the home directory
rather than the current working directory.
.PP
.I Cp,
.I mv
and
.I rm
provide the tomb option.
The deleted file is actually relinked to the directory
/usr/tomb/user-id
in the machine where the file was, if /usr/tomb exists.
.PP
Symbolic link can cross machine boundaries.
That is,
.PP
.DT
	ln -s
.I host:path
.I name
.PP
creates a symbolic link.  Then using
.I name
is identical to using
.I host:path.
If
.I path
is omitted,
.I name
links to the home directory on the machine
.I host.
.SH RESTRICTIONS
File searching
can cross machine boundaries only once.  For example, file searching
fails in the following cases:  the file name is
.I host:path
where
.I path
contains a cross-machine symbolic link, or the file name is local but
containing a link to a remote pathname which in turn contains another
cross-machine symbolic link.
.PP
.I Host
can only be one of the three machines in the Department
of Computer Sciences, Purdue University.
User must have the same login name in the remote machine.
.SH BUGS
Ln
.I file1
.I file2
(hard link) complains "cross-device link" if one of the files is
prefixed with
.I host:
but the other is not, even if the
.I host
is the local machine.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
rcsh(1R), libra.a(3R)
.PP
cat(1), chmod(1), cp(1), diff(1), mkdir(1), mv(1), rm(1), rmdir(1)
.PP
Walter F. Tichy and Zuwang Ruan,
.I "Towards a Distributed File System,"
Proceedings of USENIX 1984 Summer Conference.
